WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:02.930
Marcus Swanson, Filmmaker/LA Film School Student:
As we’ve been talking about transmedia and

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:08.220
this idea about how to pull a bunch of stories
from our story world, were do you look to

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.959
to try and craft that many stories out of
one story world?

00:00:10.959 --> 00:00:16.430
What kind of techniques do you use to pull
good stories out for all different platforms

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:17.500
across your story world?

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.689
Houston Howard, Transmedia Author and Instructor:
So people can get the theory and excited about

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:29.230
the model but practically what are the strategies
used to identify jumping off points in story

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:31.140
that maximizes the transmedia model.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:39.730
There are a couple ways that I think are very
easy to identify and one of them is unanswered

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:40.730
questions.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:44.300
And so I think this is the easiest way to
create transmedia opportunities.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:52.200
Identify unanswered questions in your script
and, in a movie, in a TV show (whatever it

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:58.500
is) and then answer them somewhere else on
a different platform.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:04.520
Take the classic example of PULP FICTION “What’s
in the briefcase?”

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:09.130
And if they didn’t answer it in the film
which I think was right for the film, it created

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:16.740
a mystique around the film, but if today Tarantino
launched a web series, or a comic book series,

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:22.200
or a novel that was all about the briefcase
and the guys who go to get the briefcase and

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.420
actually reveals what’s in the briefcase,
all of the sudden that is a tremendous incentive

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.890
of me as a fan of PULP FICTION to go check
that out.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.560
In INCEPTION where you don’t know in the
end where he is stuck in the dream or not

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.409
stuck in the dream because he spins the top
and they cut the film (it’s an unanswered

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:39.409
film).

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:48.540
And so if they answered that somewhere else
in an app or song or something else, that’s

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:49.780
a transmedia opportunity.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:55.250
I like to think back to Sophia Coppola’s
first film LOST IN TRANSLATION and at the

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.290
end Bill Murray whispers something into her
ear and you don’t know what he says.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.549
That’s an unanswered question.

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:06.560
And if 20 years or 10 years later (however
long that film has been out) if they reengage

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.620
the audience by whether it’s a love song
that reveals the line.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.791
Maybe it’s an email that Bill Murray’s
character sends Scarlett Johansson’s character

00:02:14.791 --> 00:02:20.650
that the audience is CC’d on that “Remember
that time I whispered this into your ear in

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:21.670
Tokyo?”

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.849
That all the sudden is valuable for the audience.

00:02:23.849 --> 00:02:31.159
So if you go through a script, you can readily
identify transmedia opportunities that you

00:02:31.159 --> 00:02:36.200
maybe didn’t know from the beginning of
where did that character go when she went

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.200
off screen?

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.529
Why does he have a black eye?

00:02:38.529 --> 00:02:40.269
What’s behind that door?

00:02:40.269 --> 00:02:41.510
Where does that car go?

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:43.340
What are these guys talking about?

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:50.329
Like unanswered questions, you can probably
find 15 in an existing script or pilot already

00:02:50.329 --> 00:02:55.909
that is then a transmedia opportunity that
you can split off and answer somewhere else.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:03:02.189
Or if you’re creating or writing a script
from square one with this knowledge you will

00:03:02.189 --> 00:03:08.689
naturally build in questions that you know
you’re not going to answer until you release

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:10.810
this other thing in a different platform.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:17.939
And so a great example of that is when in
THE FORCE AWAKENS in STAR WARS C3p0 has a

00:03:17.939 --> 00:03:19.099
red arm.

00:03:19.099 --> 00:03:22.680
And no one knows why C3p0 has a red arm, it’s
an unanswered question.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:28.739
Well then Lucas Film released a one-shot comic
book about C3p0 and how he got the red arm.

00:03:28.739 --> 00:03:36.370
It’s such a simple, stupid thing that is
not rocket science.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:41.769
It’s like literally play on the curiosity
of people, pose a question, don’t answer

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:43.689
it, answer it somewhere else.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.989
It’s like ready-made transmedia in a box.

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:46.989
J.j.

00:03:46.989 --> 00:03:48.279
Abrahams called it Mystery Box, right?

00:03:48.279 --> 00:03:54.290
He sort of coined this term Mystery Box of
the human mind will always want answers to

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:55.290
questions.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:04:01.710
And if you just build in intentionally questions
that don’t answer in your script, answer

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:06.639
them somewhere else, maybe not immediately,
maybe wait ten years and wait until your feel

00:04:06.639 --> 00:04:10.839
like your audience is starting to wane and
die out and this is a way to reengage them

00:04:10.839 --> 00:04:12.269
in a great way.

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:17.159
In music you have Michael Jackson “Billie
Jean is not my lover.

00:04:17.159 --> 00:04:21.139
She’s just a girl who says that I am the
one.

00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:22.259
The kid is not my son.”

00:04:22.259 --> 00:04:25.979
The unanswered question is, is Michael the
dad of this kid or isn’t he?

00:04:25.979 --> 00:04:29.120
He says he’s not but we don’t know, it’s
an unanswered question.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:35.849
So today if they answered something that revealed
whether Michael was the father of that kid,

00:04:35.849 --> 00:04:39.310
I would absolutely check that out (for sure).

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:44.800
I like to use the example of my early college
days when the Baja Men had the song “Who

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.010
let the dogs out?”

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.470
Right?

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:54.070
If today the Baja Men Tweeted out “Come
check out my new album.”

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:56.650
I would never check that link.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:58.020
I don’t listen to the Baja Men.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:04.800
But if they said “Hey, click this link and
actually find out who let the dogs out.”

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.509
I would do that, of course I would do that.

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:12.960
And then if they pay it off by going to the
website and finding out the Snoop Dog let

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.250
the dogs out and now there is a new collaboration
on the new album or whatever all the sudden

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:23.580
they got to me to their website but they got
me by playing on the curiosity of I always

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.419
wondered what the answer to that question
is.

00:05:26.419 --> 00:05:28.949
That’s a great opportunity for transmedia.

00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:33.039
I think character backstory is a great opportunity
for transmedia.

00:05:33.039 --> 00:05:36.000
You know, screenwriters deal with this all
the time, how much backstory of the characters

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.250
can we put in the script?

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.509
The more you explore backwards, the less you’re
going forward.

00:05:39.509 --> 00:05:43.970
And it’s always this push and pull in a
script because you only have a 110 pages in

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:47.629
a feature, etc., etc.

00:05:47.629 --> 00:05:51.569
If you understand the transmedia model, this
is a tremendous tool for writers to say I

00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:57.759
can still tell my backstory without overburdening
or encumbering my feature script for my TV

00:05:57.759 --> 00:06:02.319
pilot, I’m just going to tell the backstory
somewhere else in a different platform.

00:06:02.319 --> 00:06:09.289
So maybe I have my feature script, maybe I
have a short story that is a character backstory,

00:06:09.289 --> 00:06:12.870
maybe I have a song, maybe I have a song,
maybe I have an animated short film that works

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:13.979
as the backstory.

00:06:13.979 --> 00:06:32.409
Maybe I have a feature script and maybe I
have a short story.

00:06:32.409 --> 00:06:43.229
AMC does this all the time, for all the characters
all the time on the AMC website they will

00:06:43.229 --> 00:06:46.300
launch digital comics that has more of the
backstory of all their characters which is

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:52.479
extended content for the fans but for creators
it’s great because we get the things that

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:53.479
we love.

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:58.310
We get the backstory that we love but at the
same time we get a tight, efficient script

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:01.270
or TV show or movie that isn’t encumbered.

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:06.360
So that’s another really super, easy way.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:10.999
Different parts of the story world that you
can explore of you never show what’s in

00:07:10.999 --> 00:07:16.190
the basement of the house, then all of the
sudden a short story or a poem or a piece

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:21.630
of art that shows what’s in the basement
(viable comprehension), things like that which

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:28.090
are practical ways to approach transmedia
strategy that aren’t hard, that aren’t

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:31.889
rocket scientist things to figure out.

00:07:31.889 --> 00:07:37.009
Deleted scenes, I’m always a big fan of
deleted scenes of them not just dying on a

00:07:37.009 --> 00:07:38.860
DVD special feature.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:43.120
Or there are always scenes that are always
scenes that we screenwriters have to cut out

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.669
that we can’t use because we’re trying
to come in at page-length.

00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:51.009
So I’m always an advocate of not deleting
but cutting and pasting.

00:07:51.009 --> 00:07:55.530
And you can take the scenes that you cut from
a script or cut from a book or a chapter that

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:59.710
you remove from a book or a verse that you
remove from a song and just figuring out how

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:04.749
to repurpose that into a different media or
a different platform, then it’s a ready-made

00:08:04.749 --> 00:08:07.150
transmedia opportunity that is already written.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:12.320
Then maybe you figure out how to turn that
into an illustrated thing, maybe communicated

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:17.919
through a digital platform and make it a VR
opportunity or communicated through augmented

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:21.150
reality, something like that would be interesting.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:29.780
Just imagine if you had scenes that were cut
out of RESERVOIR DOGS or PULP FICTION or something

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:34.520
like this and then there was a mug that you
could buy that was a PULP FICTION mug or a

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:38.200
RESERVOIR DOGS mug that you had an augmented
reality app that you could hold up to the

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:45.330
mug and you can read or experience the scene
that was cut out of the film 20 years earlier.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:46.330
That’s awesome!

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:47.330
I would love that.

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:48.530
As a fan I would love that.

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:51.570
And then if you know that fans would love
that, then why don’t you do that.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.720
You’re wasting those scenes anyway.

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:59.650
So identifying that narrative excess, unanswered
questions, further story world exploration,

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:00.760
character backstories.

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:04.890
All great ways to have ready-made, in-the-box,
transmedia strategy.

